2026-07-26 00:55:10 | INFO     | main | [score_5d6b11f0ea5a] Manual score request | task_type=creative | model_id=manual_test_case | query='Give a creative analogy explaining how a neural network learns, for a 10-year-ol'
2026-07-26 00:55:17 | DEBUG    | main | [score_5d6b11f0ea5a] Manual score result | {'model_id': 'manual_test_case', 'relevance': 1.0, 'coherence': 1.0, 'completeness': 1.0, 'consistency': 0.12, 'weighted_score': 0.868, 'confidence_tier': 'HIGH', 'latency_ms': 0.0, 'success': True, 'error': None, 'content': 'Sure! Here\'s a fun analogy to explain how a neural network learns, just like training a super-smart robot pet:\n\n---\n\n**Imagine a neural network is like a puppy learning to fetch a ball.**\n\n1. **Random Guesses at First** – At the start, the puppy doesn’t know anything. When you throw the ball, it might run in the wrong direction, trip over its paws, or just stare at you blankly. The neural network starts the same way—it makes wild guesses!\n\n2. **Getting Corrected (Feedback)** – Every time the puppy fails (like running away from the ball), you say, *"No, silly! This way!"* and gently guide it back. The neural network gets feedback too—if it guesses wrong, it adjusts its "brain" (the math inside) to do better next time.\n\n3. **Practice Makes Perfect** – After many tries, the puppy starts running the right way because it remembers what worked before. The neural network does the same—it tweaks its connections (like adjusting strings in a puppet) until it gets the answer right most of the time.\n\n4. **Hidden Layers = More Puppies!** – A simple neural network is like one puppy learning. A deep neural network is like having a whole pack of puppies, each helping the others figure things out faster. One puppy might notice the ball’s color, another the distance, and together they make a great team!\n\n5. **Magic of Learning** – At first, the puppy is clumsy, but with practice, it becomes super fast and accurate. A neural network does the same—it starts dumb but, with enough examples, becomes a genius at recognizing patterns (like faces, words, or even cat videos!).\n\n**Final Thought:** Just like the puppy learns by playing and getting better, a neural network learns by making mistakes and improving—until it’s a pro!\n\nWould you like an even simpler version or a different analogy? 😊\n\n### Additional Insights\n* Imagine you are trying to teach a **blindfolded robot** how to sort blocks into two piles: "Good Blocks" and "Bad Blocks."\n\nThe robot doesn\'t have eyes, but it has a thousand tiny knobs on its head that it can turn left or right.\n\n### 1.\n* The Guessing Game (Forward Pass)\nYou hand the robot a block.\n* It has no idea what it is, so it randomly turns some knobs and decides, "I think this is a \'Good Block\'!" and drops it into the Good pile.\n* If the block was actually a "Bad Block," **you ring a loud buzzer.** If it was a "Good Block," **you give it a high-five.**\n\n### 2.'}
