2026-07-26 00:56:38 | DEBUG    | main | [score_72100eca357b] Manual score result | {'model_id': 'manual_test_case', 'relevance': 1.0, 'coherence': 0.8863, 'completeness': 1.0, 'consistency': 0.4, 'weighted_score': 0.8816, 'confidence_tier': 'HIGH', 'latency_ms': 0.0, 'success': True, 'error': None, 'content': 'Sure! Imagine a neural network is like a **super-smart pet dog** trying to learn how to fetch your slippers.\n\nHere’s how it works:\n\n1. **You Teach It (Training Data)** – Every time you throw a slipper, you tell the dog, *"Good job!"* if it brings back the right one (like a red slipper) or *"Nope!"* if it brings back something wrong (like your shoe).\n\n2. **It Makes Mistakes (Weights & Errors)** – At first, the dog guesses randomly—maybe it brings back your shoe instead of the slipper. But you correct it, and it learns a little bit.\n\n3. **It Adjusts Its Brain (Backpropagation)** – The dog’s brain (like the neural network’s "weights") changes slightly each time. If it keeps messing up, it figures out, *"Oh, maybe I should sniff for red first!"*\n\n4. **It Gets Better! (Learning)** – After lots of tries, the dog starts fetching the right slipper almost every time—just like how a neural network gets smarter with practice.\n\nSo, a neural network is like a dog that learns from its mistakes until it’s a pro at the game! 🐶🔥\n\n### Additional Insights\n* The "Guessing" Phase (Random Weights)\nAt the start, the robot has no idea what it’s doing.\n* You give it a bowl, some flour, and chocolate chips.\n* The robot just throws random amounts of ingredients into a bowl and tosses it in the oven.\n* It’s either a pile of salty dust or a burnt rock.'}
