2026-07-26 00:57:23 | DEBUG    | main | [score_7aaaf37e6790] Manual score result | {'model_id': 'manual_test_case', 'relevance': 1.0, 'coherence': 0.85, 'completeness': 1.0, 'consistency': 0.6, 'weighted_score': 0.9025, 'confidence_tier': 'HIGH', 'latency_ms': 0.0, 'success': True, 'error': None, 'layer2_factual': {'penalty': 0.0, 'hedge_flags': [], 'numerical_flags': [], 'hedge_count': 0, 'numerical_conflict_count': 0}, 'content': 'Leaves change color in autumn due to a combination of environmental changes and the breakdown of chlorophyll, the green pigment that drives photosynthesis. Here’s a step-by-step explanation of the process:\n\n### **1. Chlorophyll Breakdown (Green Fades)**\n- In spring and summer, chlorophyll (the pigment that makes leaves green) dominates, masking other pigments.\n- As days shorten and temperatures drop in autumn, chlorophyll production slows and eventually stops.\n- The existing chlorophyll breaks down, revealing other pigments that were present but hidden.\n\n### **2. Carotenoids (Yellows & Oranges Emerge)**\n- **Carotenoids** (yellow, orange, and brown pigments) are present in leaves year-round but are usually overshadowed by chlorophyll.\n- These pigments help capture light energy for photosynthesis and protect leaves from excess sunlight.\n- When chlorophyll fades, carotenoids become visible, giving leaves their yellow and orange hues (e.g., birch, aspen, hickory).\n\n### **3. Anthocyanins (Reds & Purples Appear)**\n- **Anthocyanins** (red, purple, and pink pigments) are not always present but are produced in some leaves in autumn due to:\n  - **Sugar trapping** – Cooler nights and sunny days trap sugars in leaves, leading to anthocyanin production.\n  - **Protective role** – These pigments may help leaves survive freezing temperatures and protect against light damage.\n- Trees like maples, oaks, and dogwoods display vibrant reds and purples because of anthocyanins.\n\n### **4. Tannins (Browns Persist)**\n- Tannins (brown pigments) are waste products that accumulate in leaves as they age.\n- When other pigments break down, tannins remain, giving some leaves a brownish tint (e.g., oak leaves).\n\n### **Why Don’t All Trees Change Color the Same Way?**\n- **Deciduous trees** (e.g., maples, oaks) lose leaves in autumn and show dramatic color changes.\n- **Evergreens** (e.g., pines, spruces) retain needles year-round and don’t change color significantly.\n- Some trees (like elms) may turn brown without vibrant colors because they lack anthocyanins.\n\n### **Factors Affecting Autumn Colors**\n- **Sunlight & Temperature** – Bright days and cool (but not freezing) nights enhance color production.\n- **Rainfall** – Drought can lead to early leaf drop with muted colors, while wet conditions prolong color.\n- **Genetics** – Some tree species are naturally more colorful than others.\n\n### **Final Stage: Leaf Drop**\n- As days shorten further, a layer of cells (**abscission layer**) forms at the base of the leaf stem, cutting off water and nutrients.\n- The leaf eventually dries out, turns brown, and falls to the ground.\n\n###\n\n### Additional Insights\n* Trees perceive these changes as a signal to prepare for winter.\n* This layer acts as a seal, gradually cutting off the flow of water and nutrients to the leaf.\n* As this happens:\n*   **Chlorophyll breaks down:** Without a constant supply of nutrients and light, the chlorophyll dies off.\n* As the leaf’s connection to the tree is cut, some sugars become trapped in the leaf.'}
